\nThanks in advance!\n\n\n\n### Answer:\n\nThe reason for the error in your query is because in MySQL you cannot SELECT\nfrom the table you are trying to UPDATE in the same query.\n\nUse UPDATE IGNORE to avoid duplicate conflicts.\n\nI think you should try reading INSERT ON DUPLICATE KEY. The idea is that you\nframe an INSERT query that always create a DUPLICATE conflict and then the\nUPDATE part will do its part.\n\n"""'}], 'response': "The root cause for such a long runtime in Case 1 is that the planner has rewritten the query embedding the view code in an inefficient way. This can be fixed by using a CTE (Common Table Expression) to force the planner to materialize the selection from the views, which should give results in acceptable times.\n\nIn Case 2, both queries are equivalent and will return the same result set. The `NOT IN` query is generally preferred because it's shorter and easier to parse.\n\nIn Case 3, the issue arises because of the way the `MINUS` operator works. When you use `MINUS`, it returns all rows from the first query that are not present in the second query. This can lead to different results if there are duplicate values in the `status_code` column. The solution is to use a subquery or a join instead of `MINUS`.\n\nIn Case 5, the issue arises because you cannot specify the target table for update in the FROM clause. To solve this problem, you should use UPDATE IGNORE to avoid duplicate conflicts. Alternatively, you can use INSERT ON DUPLICATE KEY to frame an INSERT query that always creates a duplicate conflict and then the UPDATE part will do its part.", 'time': 2.3209898471832275}
